# pytest-textual-snapshot SVG layout baselines must stay LF-only so the
# Linux-CI drift oracle compares byte-for-byte against Windows-dev checkouts
# (batch-25). Without this, core.autocrlf=true would rewrite them to CRLF on a
# Windows checkout and every CI snapshot comparison could spuriously fail.
tests/__snapshots__/**/*.svg text eol=lf
# Report byte-identity goldens (batch-35 AT-054b/AT-055b) stay LF-only for the
# same reason: the ATs undo CRLF on read, but LF-on-disk keeps the fixtures
# byte-stable across Windows/Linux checkouts.
tests/goldens/** text eol=lf

# Out-of-VCS rollback bytes (batch-66). These are byte-exact copies of files that
# live OUTSIDE this repo (~/.claude/commands/, ~/.claude/skills/, the course on G:),
# and their recorded SHA256 IS the rollback check -- there is no git history to fall
# back on. They carry MIXED line endings by nature (dev-flow.md is CRLF on this host,
# fast-dev-flow.md is LF), so normalization in EITHER direction breaks the hash and
# silently makes the rollback restore bytes that are not the originals. Store verbatim.
# (Found the hard way: the first batch-66 commit stored dev-flow.md.PRE as d46bb314...
# instead of the recorded e103af29..., under core.autocrlf=true.)
.dev-flow/**/pre-bytes/** -text
